- `branch(b1, XOR)`, `branch(b2, XOR)`, `branch(b3, XOR)`, `branch(b6, XOR)`, `branch(b10, XOR)`: Lack corresponding `guard` statements to enforce conditions explicitly stated in the source (e.g., "greater than 1000", "translation is required", "rejected" vs "approved"), leaving control-flow logic unconditioned. (SMC4)
- `defines(v1, ...)` through `defines(v10, ...)`: All variable ranges are defined as `{}`, failing to capture source-specified value domains such as numeric thresholds or binary decision sets. (SMC6)
- `action(n9, "edit the report")`, `action(n13, "edit the report")`, `action(n20, "edit the report")`: Have no outgoing `precedence` edges, creating dead-end paths that break complete init-to-end progressions. (SMC5)
- `precedence(n11, b6)`: Omits the step explicitly stated in the source ("Once again, evaluate the review decision") before branching to rejection/approval outcomes. (SMC1)
- `action(n2, "Is expense greater than 1000")`, `action(n4, "Is translation needed")`, `action(n8, "Is report rejected after review")`, `action(n10, "Is report approved after review")`, `action(n11, "check if translation is needed")`, `action(n12, "Is report rejected after second review")`, `action(n14, "Is report approved after second review")`, `action(n18, "Are receipts accepted")`: Interrogative decision points are modeled as `action` nodes rather than utilizing `guard` conditions on branches, misaligning node payload semantics with their intended control-flow function. (SMC2)

